\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Hypotheses for the derived Clifford products}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring and let #{x} be a vertex of
a free calculus. The \newvocab{direction module} #{V_x} is the
localized fibre #{e_x\Omega^1}, free on the edges leaving #{x}. A
\vocabk{quadratic form}{ca-0004} #{q_x} on #{V_x} is declared data.
The \vocabk{Clifford algebra}{ca-0006} #{\operatorname{Cl}(q_x)} is
the square quotient of \ref{fdip-000P}.}

\p{The polar form needs nothing beyond #{q_x}. As in \ref{fdip-000P},
##{B_q(u,v)=q(u+v)-q(u)-q(v)}
satisfies #{B_q(v,v)=2q(v)}.}

\p{Contraction and the scalar product need a bilinear form #{b} with
#{b(v,v)=q(v)}, or else they need #{2} to be a unit, in which case
#{b=B_q/2} is the symmetric choice. On the hyperbolic plane over
#{\mathbb Z} that symmetric form does not exist; the calculation is
\ref{fdip-00O6}. When #{q} is the standard dot product on a declared
basis, contraction is already the operator #{\iota_i} of
\ref{fdip-0092}, and #{\gamma_i=\epsilon_i+\iota_i} obeys the
Clifford relation proved there. Injectivity of that relation is
not part of the cited statement.}

\p{Grade projection, and the Chevalley identification of the exterior
algebra with #{\operatorname{Cl}(q)}, need a bilinear form #{B} with
#{B(v,v)=q(v)}. When #{2} is a unit there is one symmetric choice,
#{B=B_q/2}. Over #{\mathbb Z} the two choices in \ref{fdip-00O6}
give different scalar parts of #{e_1 e_2}.}

\p{Reversion needs no extra hypothesis. Since squares are unchanged
in the opposite algebra, the universal property in \ref{fdip-000P}
supplies an anti-automorphism which fixes #{V_x} and reverses
products.}

\p{A pseudoscalar, and the duality it determines, need #{V_x} free of
some rank #{n} together with a chosen generator of
#{\bigwedge^n V_x}. If #{k=\mathbb Z} and #{V_x=\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z}, the module
is not free, and its top exterior power is not free of rank #{1}.}

\p{A vector #{v} is a versor when #{q(v)} is a unit of #{k}. Then
#{v\cdot(q(v)^{-1}v)=1} in the Clifford algebra, so #{v} is
invertible. The hypothesis cannot be omitted: on #{\mathbb Z} with
#{q(1)=2}, the class of a generator squares to #{2} and is not
invertible in #{\mathbb Z[\sqrt{2}]}. Whether invertibility of a
vector forces #{q(v)} to be a unit for every quadratic form over
every ring is left open here; the unit hypothesis is sufficient.}

\p{The Clifford group is defined over any #{k}. The sign map
#{v\mapsto -v} preserves squares, so it extends to an automorphism
#{\alpha} by \ref{fdip-000P}. The group consists of the units #{u}
satisfying #{\alpha(u)\,V_x\,u^{-1}=V_x}.}

\p{A spinor module is taken over the commutant, and the trichotomy
needs a field. Over #{\mathbb R} the three types are the
Frobenius–Schur list of \ref{fdip-0051}, read on the
\vocabk{right multiplicity modules}{fdip-00KN}. Over a general
field the datum is the Wedderburn division algebra of a simple
module, together with the Brauer class of the Clifford algebra.
Over a ring the trichotomy is not asserted.
For a counterexample take #{k=\mathbb Z}, #{q=0} on a free module
of rank #{1}, and let #{W=\mathbb Z} on which the nilpotent
generator acts by zero. The commutant is #{\mathbb Z}, which is
none of #{\mathbb R}, #{\mathbb C}, and #{\mathbb H}.}

\transclude{fdip-00PH}
